.TH XD\-FILES 1 2026-10-20 XeonDesktop "XeonDesktop Manual"
.SH NAME
xd\-files \- the XeonDesktop file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY xd\-files
.OP \-\-explore
.OP \-\-search
.OP \-\-view view
.RI [ folder ...]
.YS
.SY xd\-files
.IR search .xdsearch
.YS
.SY xd\-files
.B \-\-properties
.IR file ...
.YS
.SY xd\-files
.RB { \-\-trash | \-\-delete }
.IR file ...
.YS
.SY xd\-files
.B \-\-rename
.I file
.YS
.SY xd\-files
.RB { \-\-copy\-to | \-\-move\-to }
.I dir
.IR file ...
.YS
.SY xd\-files
.B \-\-empty\-trash
.YS
.SY xd\-files
.RB { \-\-map\-drive | \-\-disconnect\-drive | \-\-reconnect }
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xd\-files
is the file manager, modelled on Windows XP's Explorer windows: a menu
bar, Back, Forward and Up, the Folders tree, an address bar, the five
views (Thumbnails, Tiles, Icons, List and Details) and a status bar.
It opens each
.I folder
(a path or URI) in a window of its own, or the home folder.
Each window is a process of its own.
.PP
As in XP, a folder window opens with XP's task pane on the left, and
the Folders button (or View \(-> Explorer Bar \(-> Folders) puts the
Folders tree in its place; turned off, the task pane is back.
Started without a
.IR folder ,
as XP's Windows Explorer, or with
.BR \-\-explore ,
as its Explore, the window opens with the Folders tree instead.
With Folder Options' \(lqUse Windows classic folders\(rq there is no task
pane: with Folders off, the view has the whole width.
Open windows follow that setting at once.
.PP
The task pane holds the folder's tasks (Make a new folder; Rename, Move,
Copy or Delete the selection; My Computer's System Tasks), Other Places
and Details, each group folded away by a click on its title. A theme package draws it as the
Windows style it was imported from does, and draws the toolbar, address
box, tree and status bar as Windows Vista's and 7's Explorer where it
comes from one.
.PP
A window can hold several folders in tabs, as Windows 11's File
Explorer does: File \(-> New Tab
.RB ( Ctrl+T )
opens My Computer in a new tab, the middle button opens a folder (in the
view or the Folders tree) in a new tab behind, and a folder's right-click
menu has Open in new tab.
The tab strip shows only with two or more tabs.
Each tab keeps its own folder, Back and Forward history, view,
selection and scroll position; the title bar, the taskbar button and the
address bar follow the tab in front.
.B Ctrl+W
(File \(-> Close Tab), the middle button on a tab or its close button
closes it, the window with the last one;
.BR Ctrl+Tab " and " Ctrl+Shift+Tab
go to the next and previous tab,
.BR Ctrl+1 " to " Ctrl+8
to that tab and
.B Ctrl+9
to the last.
Tabs are reordered by dragging them; a tab's right-click menu has
Duplicate tab, Close tab, Close other tabs and Close tabs to the right.
With Folder Options' \(lqOpen folders in new tabs instead of new
windows\(rq, what would open a new window from a folder window opens a
tab in it, and several
.IR folder s
open as tabs of one window.
.PP
Besides folders it opens My Computer
.RB ( computer:/// ),
the Recycle Bin
.RB ( trash:/// ,
the freedesktop.org trash, read directly),
Control Panel
.RB ( control:/// )
and My Network Places
.RB ( network:/// :
the network places added with Add Network Place, the mapped drives and
the shares open now, and Entire Network, whose Microsoft Windows Network
is gvfs's
.BR smb:/// ).
.PP
Network folders are mounted through GIO (gvfs) when they are opened,
asking for a user name and password in XP's \(lqConnect to server\(rq box;
\(lqRemember my password\(rq has gvfs keep it in the Secret Service.
They can be typed in the address bar as
.B \e\eserver\eshare
or as URIs such as
.B smb://server/share
or
.BR nfs://server/path .
Tools \(-> Map Network Drive... connects one and lists it in My Computer
under Network Drives (Linux has no drive letters); a drive mapped with
\(lqReconnect at logon\(rq is mounted again when the user logs on, and shown
with an X while it isn't connected.
Windows network folders need gvfs's SMB backend (gvfs\-smb), NFS ones its
NFS backend (gvfs\-nfs).
.PP
My Computer lists encrypted (LUKS) volumes that UDisks2 knows, locked
ones too.
Opening a locked one asks for its passphrase, unlocks it, mounts it and
opens it.
With
.B Remember my passphrase
ticked, the passphrase is kept in your keyring (the Secret Service,
through libsecret) as GNOME's Files and Disks keep it, and used from
then on without asking.
Lock, in its right-click menu, unmounts and locks it; its Properties'
Encryption tab changes the passphrase and forgets a remembered one.
.PP
A folder's Properties have XP's Sharing tab: \(lqShare this folder on the
network\(rq makes a Samba user share
.RB ( "net usershare" ),
which an administrator must have turned on (smb.conf's
.B usershare path
and
.BR "usershare max shares" ,
and membership of the group owning that folder); the tab says which is
missing.
Shared folders show XP's hand under their icons.
.PP
Where the file system keeps snapshots, a file's or folder's Properties
end with a Previous Versions tab, listing the copies in them where the
item changed, newest first: Snapper's on btrfs (asked over D-Bus; its
snapshots are root's unless its
.BR ALLOW_USERS " and " SYNC_ACL
let users read them), Timeshift's (rsync's, and btrfs's while Timeshift
has them mounted) and ZFS's
.RI ( .zfs/snapshot ).
View (Open, with the Windows 7 style) opens the copy, Copy... copies it
to a folder, and Restore, after asking, copies it back over the item; a
folder's restored contents replace those of the same name and leave the
rest.
With the Windows 7 style, an item's right-click menu has Restore
previous versions, which opens that tab.
.PP
Thumbnails missing from the freedesktop.org cache are made by
.BR xd\-thumbnailer (1).
Favorites are GTK's bookmarks, shared with file choosers.
.PP
An archive's right-click menu has Extract Here and Extract to "name"
(every format libarchive reads); what is extracted is moved into place as
a move is, asking about anything in the way, and Edit > Undo takes it
back.
Entries that would land outside the folder, device files and
set-user-ID bits are left out.
.SS Search
The Search button,
.BR F3 ,
.BR Ctrl+F " and " Ctrl+E
open Explorer's Search bar on the left, in the Folders tree's place
.RB ( F3
and the button close it again).
In the Windows XP style it is XP's Search Companion: what to search for
(Pictures, music, or video; Documents; All files and folders; Computers
or people is greyed, there being no directory to search), then the
criteria: all or part of the file name (several names apart by commas or
semicolons; * and ? wildcards; a "quoted" name as it is), a word or
phrase in the file, Look in, when it was modified (or created, or
accessed), its size, the type of file, system and hidden files,
subfolders and case.
.B Use regular expressions
(not XP's) makes the name and the phrase regular expressions.
Afterwards, "Did you find what you wanted?" refines the search, sorts
the results by category, starts a new one, or closes the bar.
Change preferences turns AutoComplete of names on or off and chooses the
standard or advanced start; there is no animated character.
With the Classic look and Start menu it is Windows 2000's Search pane:
named, containing text, Look in, Search Now and Stop Search, and Search
Options (date, type, size, and advanced options).
.PP
In the Windows 7 style the search box at the end of the address bar
searches the folder shown and its subfolders as you type, the address bar
filling in green as it goes; Esc or the box's X goes back to the folder.
Clicking it shows the recent searches and "Add a search filter": Date
modified (a calendar and 7's ranges) and Size.
Under the results, "Search again in:" searches Libraries (Documents,
Music, Pictures and Videos), Computer, a folder chosen, or the file
contents too; Save search on the command bar saves it.
The box takes Windows 7's Advanced Query Syntax:
words (all of them, in the name),
.BR AND ", " OR ", " NOT " (upper case), " \- word ,
.RB ( ... ),
"exact phrases", * and ? wildcards, and the properties
.BR name: ", " kind: " (folder, document, picture, music, video, program,"
link, email, contact, calendar),
.BR type: / ext: " (an extension, a MIME type or words of the type's name),"
.BR datemodified: ", " datecreated: ", " dateaccessed:
(today, yesterday, this week, last week, this month, last month, this
year, last year, 7's "earlier this week/month/year" and "a long time
ago", a year, or a date in the locale's format or as YYYY-MM-DD),
.B size:
(empty, tiny, small, medium, large, huge, gigantic, or a number with B,
KB, MB, GB) and
.BR content: ,
with
.BR > ", " < ", " >= ", " <= ", " = " (exact), " ~ " (contains) and " <>
and ranges
.IB a .. b\fR.
.PP
Regular expressions (not Windows'): in the box, a term written
.BI / pattern /
matches names against a Perl-compatible regular expression (PCRE, case
sensitive);
.BI / pattern /i
ignores case, and \e/ is a slash in the pattern;
.BI content:/ pattern /
matches what's in the files.
Folder Options' Search tab can turn this off.
A pattern that isn't valid says so (in the box's icon and the status bar,
or in a message from the Search bar) instead of finding nothing.
.PP
The results show in the window as Search Results (Name, In Folder, Size,
Type, Date Modified), as they are found; the part of each name that
matched is highlighted, in the theme's selected-text colours unless
Folder Options' Search tab sets a colour.
Links are not followed and no folder is searched twice; /proc, /sys,
/dev and /run are searched only from inside them, other system folders
(/usr, /etc, /var...) only with Search system folders, hidden files and
folders only when asked.
File contents are read up to 32 MiB a file: text in UTF-8, or UTF-16 with
a byte order mark, as text; anything else, as XP did, as bytes (ASCII
letters in either case, the phrase also in UTF-16LE).
With Folder Options' "Include compressed files", the names inside
archives libarchive reads are searched too (not their contents).
.PP
File > Save Search (7: Save search) saves the search in
.I ~/Searches
as a
.I .xdsearch
file, listed under Favorites; opening one searches again.
.PP
The options other than
.BR \-\-explore ", " \-\-search " and " \-\-view
do one thing without opening a folder window, and exit when it is done;
the desktop's icons use them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-explore
Open the
.IR folder s
with the Folders tree showing, as XP's Explore does, rather than the
task pane.
.TP
.B \-\-search
Open the
.I folder
(or My Computer, searching Local Hard Drives) with Search: the Search bar,
or in the Windows 7 style the focus in the search box.
The Start menus' Search runs this.
.TP
.BI \-\-view= view
Open in the view
.IR view :
.BR Thumbnails ", " Tiles ", " Icons ", " List " or " Details
(in any case).
.TP
.B \-\-properties
Show the Properties of the
.IR file s,
one dialog for them all.
.TP
.B \-\-trash
Send the
.IR file s
to the Recycle Bin, asking first.
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Delete the
.IR file s,
asking first.
.TP
.B \-\-rename
Ask for a new name for
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-\-empty\-trash
Empty the Recycle Bin, asking first.
.TP
.BI \-\-copy\-to= dir
Copy the
.IR file s
into
.IR dir ,
with the Copying... dialog and its questions.
.TP
.BI \-\-move\-to= dir
Move the
.IR file s
into
.IR dir .
.TP
.B \-\-map\-drive
Map Network Drive (the desktop's My Computer and My Network Places).
.TP
.B \-\-disconnect\-drive
Disconnect Network Drives.
.TP
.BR \-\-disk\-cleanup " [" \fIdrive\fR ]
Disk Cleanup (Start menu, System Tools; Run's \fBcleanmgr\fR): for the
drive whose root is \fIdrive\fR, or the only drive there is, else after
choosing one. Besides Windows' categories it offers graphics shader
caches, developer tools' download caches, programs' caches and unused
Flatpak runtimes; \(lqClean up system files\(rq adds the package cache,
system logs older than two weeks and crash dumps, which
.B xd\-cleanmgr\-helper
deletes through
.BR pkexec (1)
with an administrator's password.
.TP
.B \-\-reconnect
Mount the network drives mapped with \(lqReconnect at logon\(rq, without
asking for anything (a server that is away, or wants a password the
Secret Service doesn't have, is skipped), and forget those mapped without
it.
.BR xd\-shell (1)
runs it once a session.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xeondesktop/desktop.ini
Folder Options
.RB ( [Folders] ),
which open windows follow at once.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xeondesktop/network.ini
The mapped network drives (their folder, Reconnect at logon, Connect As's
user name) and the network places; never passwords.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/thumbnails/
The thumbnail cache.
.TP
.I ~/Searches/*.xdsearch
Saved searches (a key file of the criteria).
.TP
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/xeondesktop/search\-history ", " search\-names ", " search\-companion.ini
The search box's recent searches, the Search Companion's AutoComplete
names and its preferences.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XD_CLEANUP_TEMP_DIRS
Disk Cleanup's temporary folders instead of the system's (for tests):
a colon-separated list, empty for none.
.TP
.BR XD_CLEANMGR_ROOT ", " XD_CLEANMGR_TOOLS ", " XD_CLEANMGR_HELPER
For tests: a folder standing in for / for the system files, one holding
stand-ins for pacman, apt-get, dnf and journalctl, and an
.B xd\-cleanmgr\-helper
to run directly instead of through pkexec (which it ignores when run as
root).
.TP
.B XD_SEARCH_TRACE
A file each search writes to (for tests): what it found, when it started,
stopped and ended, and its errors.
.TP
.B XD_SMB_CONF
An smb.conf for
.B net
and
.B testparm
to use instead of the system's (for tests).
.TP
.B XD_SNAPSHOT_ROOTS
Where Previous Versions looks, instead of everywhere (for tests): a
colon-separated list of
.BR snapper ,
.BI zfs= mountpoint
and
.BI timeshift= backup .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xd\-folders (1),
.BR xd\-thumbnailer (1),
.BR xd\-control (1)
